  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1006 AM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nOur active monsoon pattern persists for today due to above-normal \nmoisture and the upper low lingering just to the east. PWs approach \n1.5\" with SBCAPE values around 1000 J/kg and weak steering flow, \ncombining to result in a favorable environment for heavy rainfall. \nScattered to widespread storms develop across northern areas around \nnoon, progressing to the southwest through the afternoon and\nevening. Isolated instances of flash flooding and high rain rates\nare expected, especially in SW New Mexico and Otero lowlands, \nwhere the 0z HREF focuses the highest rainfall potential. Rain \ntotals of 2-3\" are possible in the heaviest storms according to\nthe HREF and REFS. The Flood Watch has been expanded to include \nthe Gila and much of Otero County. Gusty winds to 40 mph and \nfrequent lightning are additional threats. \n\nThe upper disturbance pushes to the west for Tue as the monsoon high \nstretches into the Southern Plains. Drier air begins to move into \neastern areas around the high, lowering storm chances. Areas along \nand west of the Divide will be favored for convection on Tue with a \nlow risk of flash flooding. The flood risk out west looks lower \nthan today. Isolated storms are forecast further east as the \nmoisture erodes.\n\nThe upper high settles over the Southern Plains into the second half \nof the week, continuing the drying trend. Enough moisture will be \nleftover for isolated mtn storms each afternoon, but dry \nconditions are forecast overall. Temperatures stay cool into \nmidweek before warming to above normal later in the week as high\npressure aloft gains influence. Lowland highs climb to AOA 100 by\nthe weekend with possible Heat Advisories for the lower RGV and El\nPaso. Storm chances may increase a bit next weekend depending on \nthe location of the monsoon high and any shortwaves/moisture \nrotating around it.",
